## Objections raised and their outcome
  1 stale binary: FAILED -- sha256 1ad1568c... matches README and current cpp/ea_non_iid.
  2 harness artefact (zsh splitting / parse): FAILED for legs -i -c and -c -i -- run.sh is #!/bin/bash, reads files, greps labelled lines; my independent re-run reproduces every figure and adds the -i-alone baseline the control lacked.
  3 documented intended behaviour: FAILED -- no fork or upstream document states last-wins; usage/README say 'either'. (It IS the default getopt idiom and inherited unchanged from upstream.)
  4 needs the escape / <1e6 samples: FAILED -- mode selection happens before any data is read; -a/-t truncation can ONLY bind at >= 1,000,001 bits, i.e. compliant sizes.
  5 direction: neutral HOLDS -- each run prints the correct figure for the mode it ended in (h' 0.732612 == -c alone; H_original/min 5.860894 == -i alone); nothing is overstated by the tool.
  6 the -a -t leg: SUCCEEDED against the reproducer's wording only -- at 80000 bits -a, -t, -a -t, -t -a all print 'Number of Binary Symbols: 80000', so 'a-then-t truncates' in run.sh's PASS message is a source inference (non_iid_main.cpp:344 + last-wins), not a measurement. Measuring it needs > 125,000 8-bit samples (heavy slot).

## Verdict: CONFIRMED (core -i/-c claim), severity LOW, direction neutral; the -a/-t leg should be relabelled SOURCE-CONFIRMED until measured at compliant size.
